f movable type made from a lead-tin-alloy, cast using a hand-held mold. This allowed individual letters to be arranged, inked, and pressed onto paper efficiently. Gutenberg also adapted existing screw-press technology, likely used for wine and cloth production, and developed an oil-based ink that adhered well to paper.\n\nThe result was a dramatic reduction in the cost and time required to produce books. While a manuscript copy of the Bible might take a scribe years to complete, Gutenberg's press could produce hundreds of copies in a fraction of the time. The Gutenberg Bible, produced around 1455, stands as a masterpiece of the new technology and demonstrated its extraordinary potential.\n\n## The Rapid Dissemination of Printing\n\nThe impact of the printing press was nothing short of explosive. By the"}